    </header>

    <!-- Main Content Overlapping Hero -->
    <main class="flex-1 max-w-4xl mx-auto w-full px-6 py-8 -mt-16 relative z-20 macos-glass mb-12">
        
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4"><span class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-white/10 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center gap-1"><svg class="w-3 h-3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 8v4l3 3m6-3a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg> 2 MIN READ</span></div><div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-8 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-white/10 pb-6">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">Community</h2>
            <div class="w-24 h-1 rounded-full opacity-80" style="background-color: #10b981"></div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="prose  max-w-none">
            <div class="mb-8"><h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>Created for Connection</h3><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">Before sin entered the world, before there was brokenness or pain, God looked at Adam in a perfect garden with perfect fellowship with his Creator and said, "It is not good for man to be alone" (Genesis 2:18). Let that sink in — even in paradise, isolation was the one thing God called "not good." He wired us for deep, face-to-face, life-on-life relationship. But here's the tension we all feel: we live in the most digitally connected generation in human history, and we are lonelier than ever.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">We have hundreds of followers and no one who actually knows us. We scroll through curated highlight reels while sitting alone in our apartments wondering why we feel so empty. Social media promises connection but delivers comparison, envy, and surface-level interaction.</p><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">Biblical community is God's answer to the loneliness epidemic. It's not another app or platform — it's a table, a living room, a circle of people who know your real name, your real story, and your real struggles, and who choose to stay anyway. The early church devoted themselves to fellowship, to breaking bread together, and to prayer (Acts 2:42) — and God added to their number daily.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">That's what happens when community is real.</p></div><div class="mb-8"><h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>What Our Small Groups Look Like</h3><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Our small groups are where the Sunday sermon becomes Monday reality. Groups of 6-12 young adults gather weekly in homes across the city — sharing meals around real tables, opening Scripture together, wrestling with hard questions, and praying for one another with specificity and faith. This isn't a Bible study where one person lectures and everyone else nods politely.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">It's a family dinner where everyone has a voice, everyone is seen, and no one performs. You'll find laughter and tears in the same evening. You'll share a meal with someone and then carry their burden in prayer throughout the week.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Over time, these people stop being "your small group" and start being your people — the ones who show up when life falls apart, the ones who celebrate when God moves, the ones who hold you accountable when you're drifting. This is what the church was always supposed to look like: not a program, not an event, but a Christ-centered family doing life together in the mess and the beauty of it all.</p></div><div class="mb-8"><h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>The Power of Vulnerability</h3><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">James 5:16 gives us one of the most terrifying and transformative commands in all of Scripture: "Confess your sins to one another and pray for each other so that you may be healed." Notice the order — confession, then prayer, then healing. Most of us want the healing without the vulnerability, but God designed it to work the other way around. We stay stuck in cycles of shame, addiction, anxiety, and spiritual dryness because we refuse to let anyone see the real us.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">We curate our Christian image the same way we curate our Instagram feed — polished, filtered, and fundamentally dishonest. But healing doesn't happen in isolation. Breakthrough doesn't come from white-knuckling your sin in secret.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">It comes when you sit across from another believer and say, "I'm struggling. I need help. Pray for me." That kind of vulnerability requires trust, and trust is built over time in consistent community.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Our groups are designed to be safe spaces — not safe from truth, but safe enough to tell the truth. When you let people into your darkness, you discover that the light of Christ in them can help illuminate your path forward.</p></div><div class="mb-8"><h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>Types of Groups</h3><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">We believe there's a place for everyone, regardless of your stage of life, your schedule, or your personality. We offer college-specific groups for students navigating faith on campus, young professional groups for those balancing career and calling, co-ed groups for those who want a mixed-gender community dynamic, and men's and women's groups for those who need gender-specific spaces to go deeper on issues that require that kind of trust. Some groups focus on book studies, others walk through books of the Bible verse by verse, and some are purely fellowship-focused — doing life, serving together, and building bonds that last.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Whether you're an introvert who needs a smaller circle or an extrovert who thrives in bigger gatherings, whether you've been walking with Jesus for twenty years or you're not even sure what you believe yet — there is a seat at the table with your name on it. Don't wait until you feel ready. Community isn't something you earn; it's something you step into by faith.</p></div>
        </div>
        
